-- #1620 ON THE INTERFACE-DECLARATION AXIS.
--
-- Two interfaces in ONE file declare the same method name `ping` at DIFFERENT return
-- types, both implemented on one concrete type `T`. The must-fail pin
-- (test/must_fail_fixtures/1620-two-ifaces-same-method-binary-wrong-value/) grades the
-- AB ordering ONLY -- one `cmd:` and one `control:`, and the control spends its slot on
-- the single-interface case, so there is no slot left for the reversed ordering.
--
-- ⭐ THE REVERSED ORDERING IS UNGRADED EVERYWHERE IN THIS TREE, and grading it is why
-- this case exists: a permutation differential grades every ordering by construction,
-- where a must-fail fixture has one `cmd:` slot and one `control:` slot and #1620 has
-- already spent both.
--
-- ⚠️ A REPORTED exit-139 SEGFAULT ON THE REVERSED ORDERING DID NOT REPRODUCE on this
-- branch, and nothing here encodes it. Measured across both axes and both projections
-- (eight runs, transcribed in case.txt), the reversed interface ordering is the
-- WELL-BEHAVED one — `ping` resolves to Alpha and all three of check, the interpreter
-- and the built binary agree on `alpha`. What this case pins is an ACCEPT-vs-REJECT
-- flip, not a crash.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY `== 7` AND NOT THE RAW VALUE. Same reason the must-fail pin projects: the
-- raw form's binary prints a LIVE HEAP ADDRESS that differs on every execution, so
-- any exact-match record of it would diverge for a reason that has nothing to do
-- with interface order and would make this case red permanently and meaninglessly.
-- The Bool projection is stable. See raw-probe.mdk in the must-fail fixture.
--
-- ⚠️ NOT AN ASSERTION THAT Beta IS THE RIGHT CHOICE. Which interface `ping` belongs
-- to is the same open question as #1182's. The assertable defect is that reordering
-- two interface declarations changes no program meaning.
interface Alpha a where
  ping : a -> String

interface Beta a where
  ping : a -> Int

data T = T

impl Alpha T where
  ping _ = "alpha"

impl Beta T where
  ping _ = 7

main = println (ping T == 7)
